# ONE SOURCE FOR TERROR ATTACKS: THE TERROR DROME.
#
# John: "Should be one source. The terror drone. Terror attacks should be
# cheap and maybe they gain cobra money and influence 1 a month sounds
# good. 3 a month left alone."
#
# Before this file there were TWO sources pulling against each other:
#
#   vanilla's recurringTerror   a mission script on its own schedule, with
#                               startDelay 150 so it lands about month 5
#                               whether or not Cobra owns a single Drome
#   the Terror Drome            genMission at a daily percentage per Drome
#
# Having both makes the Drome's contribution invisible noise on top of a
# fixed schedule - flatten every Cobra site and the attacks keep coming,
# which reads as the economy not mattering. So vanilla's script goes.
# NEUTERED, NOT DELETED.
#
# firstMonth: 9999 parks the script instead of removing it. The reason is
# conservative rather than demonstrated: a save written while recurringTerror
# existed can hold one of its missions in the air -
#
#   month: 0 script: recurringTerror id: 2 type: STR_ALIEN_TERROR
#   race: STR_FLOATER region: STR_SOUTHERN_AFRICA targetZone: 3
#
# - and a live mission naming a script the ruleset no longer defines is not a
# thing to find out about from a player's save. Parking it costs nothing and
# removes the question.
#
# WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO CLAIM, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG: it said deleting the
# script HAD broken save loading, on the evidence of a 600-day campaign that
# returned nothing. That was never true. The campaign harness was loading a
# BATTLESCAPE autosave left behind by the garrison tests - -continue takes the
# newest save in the folder, autosaves included - so the geoscape was never
# reached and every run reported zeros whatever the ruleset said. The deletion
# was probably harmless. The lesson that survives is about the harness, not
# this script, and it lives in build/README.md.
missionScripts:
  - type: recurringTerror
    firstMonth: 9999

# AND THE DROME HAS TO BE AFFORDABLE, or one source means NO source.
#
# At 900 funds the Drome was the second most expensive site Cobra can build.
# Across a 400-day run it wanted one from month 11 and never bought it - the
# planner kept spending on cheaper things - so Cobra reached month 14 with no
# Drome at all. With vanilla's script deleted that would be zero terror
# attacks in the whole first year, against a calendar that wants the first
# ones at months 4 to 8.
#
# 450 puts it level with the broadcast station and below the factory, so it
# lands inside the window Cobra can actually reach early. Raise it again once
# the first-attack timing has been watched in a real run - this number is
# aimed at the calendar, not measured against it yet.
cobraSites:
  - type: STR_COBRA_TERROR_DROME
    buildCostFunds: 450

# RATE. genMissionFreq is a DAILY percentage, so the monthly count is
# roughly freq x 30 / 100 per Drome:
#
#   freq 3  ->  0.9 attacks a month per Drome   = John's "1 a month"
#   freq 4  ->  1.2                               (what shipped)
#
# "3 a month left alone" then needs no new mechanism: it is three Dromes.
# Leaving Cobra unmolested is exactly how it accumulates them, and the
# per-site genMission already scales with how many it owns.
alienDeployments:
  - type: STR_COBRA_TERROR_DROME_SITE
    genMissionFreq: 3

# WHAT COBRA BANKS FOR A MISSION NOBODY ANSWERED
#
# John: "Terror attacks should be cheap and maybe they gain cobra money and
# influence." The influence half already worked - a site that despawns adds
# alien activity to its region AND its country, which erodes funding and
# raises pact odds. This is the money half, and it is the first income in the
# economy that comes from Cobra DOING something rather than owning something.
#
# Paid ONLY when the site despawns. Raid it and Cobra banks nothing. So the
# money is something the player watched on the globe and chose not to prevent,
# which makes Cobra's war chest a running score of what he let slide.
#
# Regional, like everything else: robbing banks in North America arms North
# America, and the materiel that buys is what his men carry next time.
#
# 150 is a guess, aimed at "a Drome roughly pays for itself". At about one
# attack a month and 50 upkeep it nets Cobra ~100 a month if every attack goes
# unanswered - near a front company - and nothing at all if he answers them
# all. Raise it to make ignoring a terror site hurt more.
# WHICH DEPLOYMENT, and this was got wrong first time round.
#
# STR_COBRA_TERROR_DROME_SITE is what you fight when you raid the DROME
# ITSELF - a permanent base on the globe. It is not what a terror attack
# creates and it never despawns, so a payout there would have paid nothing,
# silently, forever.
#
# STR_ALIEN_TERROR names no siteType, so the engine takes the deployment from
# the globe TEXTURE the site lands on, and globe.rul maps those to
# STR_TERROR_MISSION. That is the thing that appears, counts down and
# despawns, so that is where the money hangs.
  - type: STR_TERROR_MISSION
    cobraPayoutFunds: 150
